# Cloudberry's singlenode_isolation2 schedule, test by test, as the port runs it.
#
#   run  <test>          runs, Cloudberry's file with Cloudberry's expected output
#   skip <test> <why>    does not, for the reason given
#
# Every test isolation2_schedule names, in its order -- the ones its "ignore:"
# lines name as well, where the schedule names a test twice at its "test:"
# line -- and parallel_retrieve_cursor_schedule's at the end, and no other; the
# lines it has commented out Cloudberry does not run either.  A test is
# skipped only for what it needs that the port's one node does not have, or
# that the port does otherwise by design; where the isolation2 suite skips the
# same test on a cluster, the reason is that manifest's.  A test whose name
# begins "port/" is the port's own (sql/port and expected/port here).
run  lockmodes
run  port/metatrack_drop
run  ao_partition_lock
run  select_dropped_table
skip prevent_ao_wal that an append-optimized table writes no WAL at wal_level = minimal, where Cloudberry, which writes and syncs such a table's files itself, logs them for its mirrors alone: gp_ao's pages are the buffer manager's and a crash is recovered from their records, so they are written there as a heap's are (the ao suite, section 19); and pg_waldump knows gp_ao's records by their resource manager's ID, where the test asks for "appendonly"
run  packcore
run  pg_views_concurrent_drop
run  drop_rename
run  resource_queue
run  bitmap_index_ao_sparse
run  pg_terminate_backend
run  reindex
skip reindex_gpfastsequence gp_fastsequence, Cloudberry's catalog of the row numbers an append-optimized table's segment files take, which gp_ao keeps in its own segment table (ao_meta.c): its REINDEX, and the INSERT that waits behind it, are append-optimized storage's own
run  instr_in_shmem_setup
run  instr_in_shmem_terminate
run  invalidated_toast_index
run  bitmap_union
skip fsync_ao a standby coordinator, which Cloudberry's single-node deployment has and the port's one node has not (see segwalrep/master_wal_switch); and the faults restartpoint_guts and ao_fsync_counter in its checkpointer: that a standby's replay hands its checkpointer the fsync of an append-optimized table's segment files, which Cloudberry writes outside the buffer manager, where gp_ao's files are pages of the table's relation in shared buffers, written and synced as any relation's (ao_storage.c), with no request of their own to count
run  disable_autovacuum
skip concurrent_index_creation_should_not_deadlock the fault before_acquire_lock_during_create_ao_blkdir_table, where Cloudberry makes an append-optimized table's block directory for its first index: gp_ao keeps every table's block directory in one table of its own, made with the extension, so a first index makes nothing; it waits for it.  The test's later form, isolation2's, on the fault defineindex_before_acquire_lock, runs as port/concurrent_index_creation_should_not_deadlock
run  port/concurrent_index_creation_should_not_deadlock
